  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively suggest that thermal or thermomechanical processing, phase and morphology control, coherent precipitates or interfaces, and dislocation-mediated deformation can influence the strength–ductility balance in high-entropy alloys. Several records concern closely related Al–Co–Cr–Fe–Ni alloys and show that annealing, homogenization, severe plastic flow, and phase-fraction control modify microstructure and properties. However, none describes HPHT treatment with a hexahedron-anvil apparatus, transformation of FCC/B2 lamellae into a hierarchical coherent-interface architecture, or preferential dislocation transfer from FCC into B2. Thus, the literature points toward microstructural and interface engineering, but selecting HPHT and the specific coherent-interface transfer mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
